# Line-ending and file-type rules, owned by the repository.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# --------------------
# Every tracked text file here is already stored LF in the index, but only
# because each developer happens to have `core.autocrlf` set. That is a
# per-machine setting, not a property of the project. Measured on the machine
# these rules were written on, `git config --show-origin --get-all
# core.autocrlf` answers `true` from
#   C:/Program Files/Git/etc/gitconfig   (system, i.e. per-installation)
#   C:/Users/<user>/.gitconfig           (user, i.e. per-developer)
# and from nothing inside this repository. A contributor who clones with it
# unset commits CRLF, and the next diff of that file is the whole file.
# Attributes move the guarantee into the repo, where it reaches everyone who
# clones it.
#
# State before these rules, from `git ls-files --eol` over all 178 tracked
# files: the `attr/` column was EMPTY for every one of them -- no attribute was
# in effect anywhere -- with 160 text files at `i/lf`, 8 auto-detected binaries
# at `i/-text`, and 10 empty files at `i/none`. In the working tree 145 were
# CRLF and 15 were LF, which is the mixture that produces the warning below.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- text default
# Everything Git detects as text: LF in the index, and LF in the working tree.
#
# `eol=lf` is the half that is easy to leave out and is doing real work.
# `* text=auto` on its own guarantees LF in the index, which is the repo-level
# guarantee -- but it leaves the checkout conversion in place, so on Windows the
# working tree still gets CRLF and `git add` still prints
#
#   warning: in the working copy of '<path>', LF will be replaced by CRLF
#   the next time Git touches it
#
# which is the other symptom this file was written to remove. Both spellings
# were measured in a throwaway clone against a file rewritten to LF: under
# `* text=auto` alone the warning still fired; under `eol=lf` it stopped.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO, MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. It rewrites nothing that
# is already committed: all 160 text files are LF in the index today, so
# `git add --renormalize .` under these rules produces zero changes, and adding
# this file left `git status` clean. Working-tree files are converted the next
# time Git writes them -- a fresh clone, a checkout, a reset -- not now.
#
# The consequence is a transition, and the honest form of the claim above has
# three cases rather than one:
#
#   * A FRESH CLONE under these rules gets LF on disk for every text file.
#     Editing one and `git add`ing it prints nothing. This is the end state and
#     it is the one the warning was about.
#   * AN EXISTING WORKING TREE still holds CRLF for files checked out before
#     this file existed. Editing one of those now prints the SAME warning with
#     the direction reversed -- "CRLF will be replaced by LF" -- until that file
#     is next written by Git. Measured on this repository: `git diff` on an
#     edited document prints exactly that line. It is transitional, not a new
#     defect, and `git status` stays clean throughout.
#   * A `.ps1` HELD AT CRLF by the exception below still warns if some tool
#     writes LF into it, because that is a real conversion the exception asks
#     for. Measured: appending an LF-terminated line to
#     `scripts/reproduce_results.ps1` in a fresh clone printed "LF will be
#     replaced by CRLF". Two tracked files can do this.
#
# The cost, stated plainly: after the transition a Windows checkout holds LF on
# disk rather than CRLF. Python, Git, Streamlit, VS Code and PowerShell all read
# LF; a tool that cannot is the reason the exception section below exists.
* text=auto eol=lf

# ------------------------------------------------- exceptions that keep CRLF
# The two tracked PowerShell scripts (`scripts/reproduce_results.ps1`,
# `scripts/build_vendor.ps1`) are the scripts a judge or a presenter runs on
# Windows, and this keeps them on disk in the byte form they have today.
#
# STATED HONESTLY, BECAUSE THE OBVIOUS JUSTIFICATION IS THE ONE THAT DID NOT
# SURVIVE MEASUREMENT: this exception is NOT repairing a failure that was
# reproduced. Both scripts were checked out LF-only in a fresh clone with this
# section deleted, and
# `[System.Management.Automation.Language.Parser]::ParseFile` under Windows
# PowerShell 5.1.26100.9444 returned 0 parse errors for each. An LF-only
# `.cmd` with labels, `call` and a parenthesised block also ran correctly under
# cmd.exe on the same machine. So the claim this rule can carry is the smaller
# one: it holds the Windows scripts at the endings Windows tooling emits, and
# it keeps a future Authenticode signature block -- which is computed over the
# file's bytes -- from being invalidated by a checkout. It is not a fix for a
# break measured here, and it should not be described as one.
*.ps1  text eol=crlf
*.psm1 text eol=crlf
*.psd1 text eol=crlf

# ------------------------------------ never convert, never diff, never merge
# `binary` expands to `-text -diff -merge`: no end-of-line conversion in either
# direction, and no attempt to treat the bytes as lines.
#
# These are NOT redundant with Git's auto-detection, and the reason is the
# whole point of the section. Auto-detection is a heuristic over the first 8000
# bytes: a file with no NUL byte in that window is treated as text. It happens
# to classify all 8 binaries tracked here correctly today, which is exactly
# what makes omitting these rules easy to argue for and expensive later.
#
# Measured against a plausible break -- a capture of plain-text HTTP with no
# NUL byte in its first 8000, which is the next thing `capture/capture.sh`
# would record: under `* text=auto eol=lf` with no rule below, `git add`
# classified it as text and the blob Git stored was 540 bytes SHORTER than the
# file on disk, one byte per CR, with no error and only a warning that read
# like a formatting note. With `*.pcap binary` the same file round-tripped
# byte-identical. A file type whose bytes are not lines belongs in this list.
*.pcap   binary
*.pcapng binary
*.cap    binary

*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.webp binary

*.pdf  binary
*.pptx binary
*.docx binary
*.xlsx binary

*.zip binary
*.gz  binary
*.xz  binary
*.whl binary

*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary

# Model artifacts. `.gitignore` keeps these out of the repository and CLAUDE.md
# forbids committing them, so none is tracked and none should be. The rules are
# here for the case where that is bypassed with `git add -f`: a weight file
# silently altered on checkout fails its SHA-256 in
# `scripts/verify_weights.py`, a long way from the commit that caused it.
*.pkl    binary
*.joblib binary
*.pt     binary
*.pth    binary
*.onnx   binary
*.npy    binary
*.npz    binary

